    <body>
      <sec id="article-24248.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pediculosis, or lice infestation, affects the hair, body, and pubic region and remains a common condition worldwide, particularly among children in communal settings. This activity explores the epidemiology, clinical presentation, and management of infestations caused by <italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus capitis</italic> (head lice), <italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus corporis</italic> (body lice), and <italic toggle="yes">Pthirus pubis</italic> (pubic lice). Learners will review the pathophysiology of pruritus induced by lice salivary proteins, the potential for body lice to transmit infectious agents, and diagnostic strategies, including direct visualization and dermoscopy. Evidence-based treatment approaches, including topical permethrin, pyrethrin with piperonyl butoxide, oral ivermectin, mechanical removal, and environmental control measures, are discussed. Participants will gain practical knowledge to identify, treat, and prevent reinfestation. By engaging in this activity, interprofessional healthcare teams enhance their competence in providing coordinated, effective patient care for this prevalent parasitic condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the epidemiology and population risk factors for pediculosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess patients using appropriate history, physical examination, and diagnostic evaluation for pediculosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate among the pharmacologic and nonpharmacologic treatment options for pediculosis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement interprofessional team strategies to enhance care coordination, facilitate early detection, and optimize treatment of pediculosis.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=24248&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=24248">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Each year, pediculosis (lice infestation) affects millions of people worldwide across all regions and socioeconomic groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r1">[1]</xref> Lice are obligate parasitic insects with no free-living stage in their life cycle. Transmission occurs via direct skin-to-skin contact or through fomites, and symptoms typically do not appear for 3 to 4 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r2">[2]</xref> The three human-parasitic species are&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus capitis</italic>&#x000a0;(head lice),&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus corporis</italic>&#x000a0;(body lice), and <italic toggle="yes">Pthirus pubis</italic>&#x000a0;(pubic or crab lice). Head lice are the most common species and affect individuals across all socioeconomic backgrounds. Body lice are more frequently found in homeless or displaced populations and should be suspected in cases of scratching, poor hygiene, or during colder months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;(See <bold>Image.</bold> Head Lice)</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Body and head lice measure 1 to 3 mm in length, while pubic lice are smaller.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;The head louse is an obligate parasite that spends its entire life on human hosts and feeds exclusively on blood. Lice cannot jump or fly, so transmission requires close contact. Head lice are typically spread through head-to-head contact, sharing of hats or hair accessories, or contact with contaminated fomites (inanimate objects that harbor the organism, such as movie seats).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r5">[5]</xref> Pubic lice are usually transmitted through sexual contact, bedding, or clothing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r5">[5]</xref> Body lice live and lay eggs in clothing or bedding and move to the skin only to feed.&#x000a0;Lice are transmitted primarily through direct contact among individuals with poor hygiene, but they can also spread via contaminated clothing, bedding, or towels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Studies on pediculosis are limited, and further epidemiologic research is needed, particularly in regions with high poverty and low health standards.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Each year, louse infestations affect hundreds of millions of people worldwide and have been reported in all countries and socioeconomic groups.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Pediculus humanus capitis</italic>&#x000a0;is estimated to affect 6 to 12 million people in the United States annually, although the exact number is unknown because it is not a reportable disease. Head lice outbreaks are most common in children aged 3 to 12, with girls affected more frequently than boys.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r7">[7]</xref> In industrialized countries, small epidemics often occur in schoolchildren, whose close social interactions facilitate rapid spread. Multiple household members may also be affected. Infestations are more frequent in warmer months and in areas of higher humidity. Head lice affect all socioeconomic groups, whereas body lice are more common among homeless or displaced populations and during colder months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r8">[8]</xref> Pubic lice infestations are most common in sexually active individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Patients typically present with pruritus resulting from an immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction. Symptoms may take 2 to 6 weeks to develop after the first exposure, whereas pruritus can appear within 1 to 2 days upon re-exposure. Scratching induced by itching can lead to secondary bacterial infections, which may result in complications such as impetigo and pyoderma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Body lice can transmit trench fever, relapsing fever, and epidemic typhus to humans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histologic analysis of the skin or hair is rarely required. When performed, skin biopsy may reveal sharp wedge-shaped epidermal changes, lymphocytic infiltrates, excoriation marks, and intradermal hemorrhage.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The primary presenting complaint of pediculosis is pruritus. On physical examination, diagnosis is confirmed by visualizing at least one louse. The use of a bright light, a magnifying lens, or a fine-toothed comb (lice comb) can facilitate detection. Misdiagnosis is common. Finding only nits does not confirm an active infestation, as nits may remain attached to hair for months after successful treatment.</p>
        <p>Additionally, dandruff, hairspray residue, and dirt particles can mimic nits, leading to false-positive findings. When lice are identified in one household member, all&#x000a0;other household members should be examined for infestation. Pubic lice are diagnosed by locating lice in the pubic hair and should prompt screening for other sexually transmitted infections. Body lice should be suspected in patients with pruritus who live in crowded conditions or exhibit poor hygiene.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of pediculosis is made clinically in most cases. Sticky tape can be applied to the affected area to collect adult lice for microscopic examination. Nits are oval-shaped and can be found firmly attached to hair shafts. Examination with a Wood lamp may reveal greenish-yellow fluorescence of the nits. Detection of lice in the pubic hair should prompt evaluation for other sexually transmitted infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r11">[11]</xref> Skin scrapings are sometimes performed to rule out fungal infections when the diagnosis is uncertain.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Historically, head lice have been removed manually by shaving affected areas or by physically removing them with a lice comb. Wet combing involves moistening the hair and combing from root to tip with a fine-toothed comb.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r12">[12]</xref> Cure rates with this method vary widely.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r13">[13]</xref> Pharmacologic treatment targets two mechanisms: neurotoxicity resulting in paralysis of the louse and suffocation from topical agents. Available treatments effectively kill lice but do not reliably destroy eggs; therefore, repeat treatment is often required. A second application, 7 to 10 days after the initial treatment, is typically sufficient to eradicate most nonresistant lice.</p>
        <p>Multiple topical pediculicides are considered first-line therapies, including pyrethroids, malathion, lindane, benzyl alcohol, topical ivermectin, and spinosad.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r14">[14]</xref> Pyrethroids are among the most commonly used agents. Permethrin 1%, a synthetic pyrethroid, is widely available over the counter in the United States and disrupts sodium transport across neuronal membranes, resulting in paralysis of the lice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r15">[15]</xref> Resistance to permethrin has been increasingly reported. It remains on the hair after application and provides residual activity for up to 3 weeks; however, retreatment at 7 days is recommended. Despite emerging resistance, its favorable safety profile and low toxicity support its use as a first-line agent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Malathion 0.5% is an organophosphate cholinesterase inhibitor that causes respiratory paralysis in lice. Although generally safe, it has an unpleasant odor and requires prolonged application (8-12 hours). Lindane 1% is an organochloride that also causes paralysis of the lice but is systemically absorbed and associated with neurotoxicity. It should not be used repeatedly and is contraindicated in children, older adults, and individuals weighing less than 50 kg. It has been removed from the market in some regions, including California. Benzyl alcohol 5% lotion, approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in 2009, acts by suffocating the lice and preventing them from closing their respiratory spiracles. Its efficacy is comparable to pyrethrins. Spinosad 0.9%, approved by the&#x000a0;FDA in 2011, causes neuronal hyperexcitation and paralysis and has demonstrated higher eradication rates than permethrin at 14 days, often with a single application.</p>
        <p>Topical ivermectin 0.5% increases chloride permeability in muscle cells, leading to hyperpolarization and paralysis of the lice. It is effective with a single application. Systemic absorption is minimal; however, it is often reserved for cases in which first-line therapies fail, in part due to cost and prescribing patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r16">[16]</xref> Oral ivermectin is the only systemic therapy currently used for pediculosis. Although effective in clinical trials, it is not approved by the&#x000a0;FDA for this indication and carries a risk of neurotoxicity. It may be considered for resistant cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Body lice are managed through improved hygiene, laundering, and, if necessary, treating clothing and bedding with insecticides. Pubic lice are generally treated with the same agents used for head lice, although sensitivity may vary. Permethrin is preferred, whereas lindane should be reserved for resistant cases due to neurotoxicity risk. Malathion has been used but is not FDA-approved for this indication. Because lice can persist on fomites, infested clothing and bedding should be washed in hot water. Temperatures of at least 52 &#x000b0;C for 30 minutes are required to kill lice and nits; however, some lice may demonstrate heat tolerance at higher temperatures.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis for pediculosis includes dandruff, seborrhea, superficial fungal infections, eczema, folliculitis, and scabies.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of pediculosis is generally favorable. When used appropriately, available treatments are highly effective in eradicating nymphs and adult lice. Treatment failure may result from several factors, including limited ovicidal activity, failure to remove viable nits, nonadherence (particularly failure to complete retreatment at 7 to 10 days), inadequate application of pediculicidal agents (eg, insufficient duration or quantity), failure to treat close contacts, inadequate environmental decontamination, and resistance to therapy. In patients with body lice, louse-borne infections such as trench fever, epidemic typhus, and relapsing fever may occur but are uncommon.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications associated with pediculosis include social embarrassment, missed school days, and loss of skin integrity, which can lead to secondary bacterial infections such as impetigo and pyoderma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;In cases of body lice infestation, louse-borne infections such as trench fever, relapsing fever, and epidemic typhus may be transmitted.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r4">[4]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Treatment failure is frequently due to nonadherence. Patients should be educated on the proper application of medications, including the appropriate amount and duration of treatment, as well as the importance of retreatment in 7 to 10 days. Patients and caregivers should also be instructed to wash infested bedding, clothing, and towels in hot water and to dry them on a high-heat setting. Both patients and caregivers should be advised to avoid sharing headgear, such as hats and hair accessories. Measures to prevent reinfestation of body lice include maintaining proper hygiene, changing clothing at least weekly, and laundering clothing and bedding appropriately. Sexual partners of patients with pubic lice should also be treated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Head lice infestation can cause psychological distress, as affected individuals may be perceived as unclean. Lice have existed for thousands of years, and pediculosis remains a common condition. Treatment failure is frequent and is most often due to inappropriate therapy, nonadherence, failure to remove viable nits, sharing of personal items, failure to treat close contacts, drug resistance, and inadequate environmental decontamination.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-24248.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Pediculosis&#x000a0;is a major public health concern affecting millions and is best managed through an interprofessional team approach. Infestations are common in schools, and diagnosis and treatment often are delayed, allowing the cycle of transmission to continue. While clinicians in emergency and primary care settings manage lice, nurses and pharmacists play critical roles in prevention and education. Pharmacists should counsel families on proper medication use and adherence, while nurses monitor follow-up, verify treatment compliance, answer questions, and report concerns to the prescriber.</p>
        <p>Effective management relies on patient and caregiver education, emphasizing that all members of an infected household should be examined and treated. Even individuals without visible lice who share personal items or bedding may require prophylactic treatment. Evidence shows that education reduces lice prevalence in schools and is more effective than &#x0201c;no nit&#x0201d; school policies. Families should also be instructed to avoid sharing combs, brushes, hats, or scarves. Although shaving the hair can eradicate lice, it is generally not recommended. For pubic lice, both sexual partners should be treated, and screening for other sexually transmitted infections is advised. Proper laundering of clothing, bedding, and linens is essential.</p>
        <p>A coordinated interprofessional approach ensures that steps to eradicate lice are implemented effectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r19">[19]</xref> Treatment is highly effective, particularly when adult lice are present; however, current therapies do not reliably kill nits, so retreatment is often necessary. Repeated infestations and treatment can cause severe pruritus, potentially leading to skin breakdown and secondary bacterial infections. Lice infestations also carry a social stigma that can contribute to embarrassment and psychological distress.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-24248.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
